+1 to the initial code being reasonable. I would probably write this as:

```ts
checkedItems = linkedSignal({
  source: this.data,
  computation: (data, previous) => {
    if (!previous || data !== previous.source) {
      return [];
    }

    return previous.value.filter((checkedItem) => this.filteredData().includes(checkedItem));
  },
});
```

> +1 to the initial code being reasonable. I would probably write this as:
> 
> checkedItems = linkedSignal({
>   source: this.data,
>   computation: (data, previous) => {
>     if (!previous || data !== previous.source) {
>       return [];
>     }
> 
>     return previous.value.filter((checkedItem) => this.filteredData().includes(checkedItem));
>   },
> });

@alxhub  I might be wrong here but I think that won't work... At the first time filteredData won't be accessed so any changes to it won't invoke the signal. That's why I used an array as source to begin with.
Edit: [stackblitz](https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-aqp9lmyf?file=src%2Fmain.ts)

